              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "nCrkk",
                  "name": "Title",
                  "fill": "$color-text-primary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "What follows from the rule",
                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "HPF02",
                  "name": "Body",
                  "fill": "$color-text-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "A filtered view never gets a breadcrumb. Breadcrumbs express containment, and a filter is not containment — showing Accounts › Current account › Transactions would claim the list lives under the account, which is exactly the thing the rule denies. The filter chip carries that context instead, and removing it widens the list rather than navigating anywhere.\n\nThe sidebar marks where you are, not how you arrived. Landing on filtered transactions from a budget still marks Transactions current, because that is the page you are on. Marking Budgets would make the sidebar a history rather than a location.\n\nDepth is counted from the documented root, not from URL segments. /transactions?account=current is depth 1, not depth 3.\n\nAnd a nested resource proves itself by the test, not by convenience: if it can be listed on its own, nesting it is a choice that costs a duplicate page.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.55,
                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
