                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": "$type-size-heading-sm",
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "ErE0t",
                  "name": "Contract",
                  "fill": "$color-text-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Where the subsections the sidebar refuses actually live: stable subdivisions of one area, on the page that owns them. Two forms chosen by count — tabs for a fixed handful of peer views, a list when the set is longer or grows. Rows are Nav item instances with the icon switched off, and that is a contract, not an oversight: a primary nav icon has to carry meaning alone because the rail strips labels, while a secondary item never collapses and its icon would never have to work. Subdivisions like Profile, Connections, Rules and Export have no honest icon, and an arbitrary one slows scanning. Text-only is also how you can tell at a glance which level you are reading.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.45,
                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": "$type-size-body-sm",
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "i37OxU",
              "name": "Specimens",
              "width": "fill_container",
