              "name": "Spec header",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "layout": "vertical",
              "gap": 3,
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "d0QN6H",
                  "name": "Name",
                  "fill": "$color-text-primary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Secondary nav form and placement",
                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": "$type-size-heading-sm",
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "ObnIu",
                  "name": "Contract",
                  "fill": "$color-text-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "The list form and the tab form are not two renderings of one thing — they answer to different content, and the choice is made once by that content, never by the regime. A fixed handful of peer views is tabs, horizontal, in the page header, at every size. A longer or growing set of subdivisions is a list, and that list is a card inside the page, under the header that names the area it belongs to, at every size. Neither form is promoted by width. A subsection list never becomes a second full-height column beside the sidebar: the sections belong to the page, and a column outside the page header claims they are a peer of the primary nav.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.45,
                  "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
                  "fontSize": "$type-size-body-sm",
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "K0cFeu",
              "name": "Specimens",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "fill": "$color-bg-canvas",
