          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "k8Jgd",
              "name": "Title",
              "fill": "$color-text-primary",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Why these are scaffolds and not screens",
              "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
              "fontSize": 12,
              "fontWeight": "600"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "EGc0F",
              "name": "Body",
              "fill": "$color-text-secondary",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "A template is regions and slots — it shows how the shell and the navigation layers compose, and stops there. The moment real merchants, balances and statuses appear, it is a Page, and a Page belongs to a journey file where its states, failures and routes live beside it. Keeping business content out of the library is not pedantry: a library full of example data drifts from the product, and nobody can tell which parts are contract and which were illustration.\n\nThe navigation question these answer: primary nav is always present, a page header always names where you are, and secondary nav appears only in areas that have subdivisions. Transactions has none, so its peer views are tabs in the header. Settings has six and growing, so it gets a list in the page — and at Ultra that list may promote to a column.",
              "lineHeight": 1.55,
              "fontFamily": "$type-family-body",
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
